This document contains TypeScript rules and best practices for the One Percent Better React Native application. TypeScript is the **primary language** for this project - all code should be written in TypeScript.

## TypeScript Configuration

- Use strict TypeScript configuration (`strict: true` in `tsconfig.json`)
- Enable all recommended type checking options
- Use path aliases for cleaner imports (e.g., `@/components`, `@/utils`)

## General TypeScript Best Practices

### Type Annotations

- Always provide explicit return types for functions
- Use type inference for simple variables when types are obvious
- Prefer interfaces for object shapes, types for unions/intersections

```typescript
// Good - Explicit return types
export function formatDate(date: Date): string {
	return date.toISOString();
}

export async function getTasks(): Promise<Task[]> {
	// ...
}

// Good - Type inference for simple cases
const count = 0; // inferred as number
const title = "Task"; // inferred as string

// Good - Interface for object shape
interface Task {
	id: string;
	title: string;
	description?: string;
	completed: boolean;
}

// Good - Type for union
type TaskStatus = 'pending' | 'completed' | 'skipped';
```

### Variable Declarations

- Use `const` for values that don't change
- Use `let` only when reassignment is necessary
- Never use `var` (deprecated)
- Provide explicit types when inference is unclear

```typescript
// Good - Using const with explicit types when needed
const DEFAULT_SNOOZE_MINUTES: number = 10;
const task: Task = { id: '1', title: 'Task', completed: false };

// Good - Using let only when reassignment is necessary
let retryCount = 0;
let unprocessedTasks: Task[] = [];

// Bad - Using var (deprecated)
var count = 0;

// Bad - Using let when value never changes
let taskTitle = 'Task';
```

### Constants

- Use **UPPER_CASE** for numeric constants
- Group related constants in objects or enums
- Use `as const` for literal types when needed

```typescript
// Good - Named constants
const DEFAULT_SNOOZE_MINUTES = 10;
const MAX_RETRIES = 3;

// Good - Grouped constants
export const TASK_CONSTANTS = {
	DEFAULT_SNOOZE_MINUTES: 10,
	MAX_RETRIES: 3,
} as const;

// Good - Enum for related values
export enum TaskType {
	DAILY = 'daily',
	WEEKLY = 'weekly',
	SINGLE = 'single',
}
```

## React Native TypeScript Patterns

### Component Props

- Always define TypeScript interfaces for component props
- Use `React.FC` or explicit function signatures
- Make optional props explicit with `?`

```typescript
// Good - Interface for props
interface TaskCardProps {
	task: Task;
	onComplete: (taskId: string) => void;
	onSkip?: (taskId: string) => void;
}

// Good - Function component with explicit props
export default function TaskCard({ task, onComplete, onSkip }: TaskCardProps) {
	// ...
}

// Good - Using React.FC (alternative)
export const TaskCard: React.FC<TaskCardProps> = ({ task, onComplete, onSkip }) => {
	// ...
}
```

### React Hooks

- Type hook return values explicitly
- Use generic types for `useState` when needed
- Type custom hooks properly

```typescript
// Good - Typed useState
const [tasks, setTasks] = useState<Task[]>([]);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState<boolean>(false);

// Good - Custom hook with return type
function useTasks(): { tasks: Task[]; loading: boolean; error: Error | null } {
	const [tasks, setTasks] = useState<Task[]>([]);
	const [loading, setLoading] = useState<boolean>(false);
	const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null);
	
	return { tasks, loading, error };
}
```

### Event Handlers

- Type event handlers properly
- Use React Native event types

```typescript
// Good - Typed event handlers
const handlePress = (): void => {
	// ...
};

const handleTextChange = (text: string): void => {
	// ...
};

// Good - React Native event types
import { NativeSyntheticEvent, TextInputChangeEventData } from 'react-native';

const handleInputChange = (e: NativeSyntheticEvent<TextInputChangeEventData>): void => {
	const text = e.nativeEvent.text;
	// ...
};
```

## React Navigation TypeScript

### Navigation Types

- Define navigation param lists
- Type navigation props and route params

```typescript
// Good - Navigation param list
export type RootStackParamList = {
	Home: undefined;
	CreateTask: { taskId?: string };
	Statistics: undefined;
	Settings: undefined;
};

// Good - Typed navigation props
import { StackScreenProps } from '@react-navigation/stack';

type HomeScreenProps = StackScreenProps<RootStackParamList, 'Home'>;

export default function HomeScreen({ navigation, route }: HomeScreenProps) {
	// navigation and route are properly typed
}
```

### Navigation Hooks

- Use typed navigation hooks

```typescript
// Good - Typed navigation hook
import { useNavigation } from '@react-navigation/native';
import type { NativeStackNavigationProp } from '@react-navigation/native-stack';

type NavigationProp = NativeStackNavigationProp<RootStackParamList>;

export default function TaskCard({ taskId }: { taskId: string }) {
	const navigation = useNavigation<NavigationProp>();
	
	const handlePress = () => {
		navigation.navigate('CreateTask', { taskId });
	};
}
```

## Realm Database TypeScript

### Realm Schema Types

- Define TypeScript interfaces for Realm schemas
- Use Realm's `Realm.Object` base class
- Type Realm query results

```typescript
// Good - Realm schema with TypeScript
import Realm from 'realm';

export interface ITask {
	_id: Realm.BSON.ObjectId;
	title: string;
	description?: string;
	type: 'daily' | 'weekly' | 'single';
	time: string;
	isActive: boolean;
	createdAt: Date;
	updatedAt: Date;
}

export class Task extends Realm.Object<Task> implements ITask {
	_id!: Realm.BSON.ObjectId;
	title!: string;
	description?: string;
	type!: 'daily' | 'weekly' | 'single';
	time!: string;
	isActive!: boolean;
	createdAt!: Date;
	updatedAt!: Date;

	static schema: Realm.ObjectSchema = {
		name: 'Task',
		primaryKey: '_id',
		properties: {
			_id: 'objectId',
			title: 'string',
			description: 'string?',
			type: 'string',
			time: 'string',
			isActive: 'bool',
			createdAt: 'date',
			updatedAt: 'date',
		},
	};
}
```

### Realm Operations

- Type Realm write operations
- Type query results

```typescript
// Good - Typed Realm operations
export async function getTasks(realm: Realm): Promise<Task[]> {
	const tasks = realm.objects<Task>('Task');
	return Array.from(tasks);
}

export async function createTask(realm: Realm, taskData: Omit<ITask, '_id' | 'createdAt' | 'updatedAt'>): Promise<Task> {
	let task: Task;
	realm.write(() => {
		task = realm.create<Task>('Task', {
			...taskData,
			_id: new Realm.BSON.ObjectId(),
			createdAt: new Date(),
			updatedAt: new Date(),
		});
	});
	return task!;
}
```

## Expo Notifications TypeScript

### Notification Types

- Type notification content and triggers
- Use Expo notification types

```typescript
// Good - Typed notification scheduling
import * as Notifications from 'expo-notifications';

interface ScheduleNotificationParams {
	title: string;
	body?: string;
	data?: Record<string, any>;
	trigger: Date | Notifications.NotificationTriggerInput;
}

export async function scheduleNotification(
	params: ScheduleNotificationParams
): Promise<string> {
	return await Notifications.scheduleNotificationAsync({
		content: {
			title: params.title,
			body: params.body,
			data: params.data,
		},
		trigger: params.trigger,
	});
}
```

## Error Handling

### Typed Error Handling

- Use typed error handling
- Create custom error classes when needed

```typescript
// Good - Typed error handling
try {
	const tasks = await getTasks(realm);
	// ...
} catch (error) {
	if (error instanceof Error) {
		console.error('Error:', error.message);
		// Handle error
	} else {
		console.error('Unknown error:', error);
	}
}

// Good - Custom error class
export class TaskNotFoundError extends Error {
	constructor(taskId: string) {
		super(`Task with ID ${taskId} not found`);
		this.name = 'TaskNotFoundError';
	}
}
```

## Utility Functions

### Generic Functions

- Use generics for reusable utility functions

```typescript
// Good - Generic utility function
export function findById<T extends { id: string }>(
	items: T[],
	id: string
): T | undefined {
	return items.find(item => item.id === id);
}

// Usage
const task = findById(tasks, '123');
```

## Best Practices Summary

1. **Types**: Always provide explicit return types, use interfaces for objects
2. **Variables**: Use `const` by default, `let` only when necessary, never `var`
3. **Constants**: Name numeric constants with UPPER_CASE, use enums for related values
4. **Components**: Define TypeScript interfaces for all component props
5. **Hooks**: Type hook return values and state variables
6. **Navigation**: Define navigation param lists and type navigation props
7. **Realm**: Define interfaces for Realm schemas, type all Realm operations
8. **Errors**: Use typed error handling, create custom error classes when needed
9. **Generics**: Use generics for reusable utility functions
10. **Strict Mode**: Always use strict TypeScript configuration
